From 8d442a5b5d42129d5fb9a8f81dab9d102a019ed1 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: =?UTF-8?q?Silas=20M=C3=BCller?= <dev@silasmueller.de>
Date: Wed, 23 Sep 2026 15:45:00 +0200
Subject: [RFC PATCH] ACPI: x86: honour _DMA translation for device DMA setup

ACPI lets a bus device describe, in its _DMA object, the child-side
address ranges it forwards to memory and the offset (_TRA) it adds to
them (ACPI 6.5, sections 6.2.4 and 6.4.3.5.1). Linux parses that object
in acpi_dma_get_range() and turns it into a struct bus_dma_region table,
but only architectures that implement acpi_arch_dma_setup() use it:

  acpi_dma_configure_id()                      drivers/acpi/scan.c
    acpi_arch_dma_setup(dev)
      arm64:     drivers/acpi/arm64/dma.c      (since v5.14)
      LoongArch: arch/loongarch/kernel/dma.c   (since v6.1)
      others:    empty inline in include/linux/acpi.h

x86 has neither the function nor a caller of acpi_dma_get_range(), so on
x86 a _DMA object is evaluated by nobody. This is historical rather than
deliberate: x86 platforms have described DMA translation through the
IOMMU tables (DMAR, IVRS), not through bus offsets. The hook was added
for arm64 by commit db59e1b6e492 ("ACPI: arm64: Move DMA setup
operations out of IORT") and given its current form, a full range table,
for LoongArch by commit bf2ee8d0c385 ("ACPI: scan: Support multiple DMA
windows with different offsets"); x86 had no user for it either time. The
x86 DMA layer itself already supports per-device translation tables:
dma-direct, including swiotlb, applies dev->dma_range_map in
phys_to_dma()/dma_to_phys() on every architecture without
ARCH_HAS_PHYS_TO_DMA, and arch/x86/pci/sta2x11-fixup.c has installed one
through dma_direct_set_offset() for years.

The missing piece matters for virtual machines. A VMM that exposes a
device whose DMA is performed by hardware in a host IOMMU domain, at I/O
virtual addresses the VMM does not choose, has to tell the guest at
which bus address each range of guest RAM is visible to the device. A
_DMA object on the PCI host bridge is the firmware interface for exactly
that, and arm64 guests already honour it.

Implement acpi_arch_dma_setup() for x86 the way LoongArch does: if
acpi_dma_get_range() finds a _DMA object on the device or an ancestor,
install the table as dev->dma_range_map, set bus_dma_limit to the
highest bus address it covers and clamp the default masks to it. Like
arm64, leave an already installed dma_range_map alone.

What changes:
- Only devices below an ACPI node with a _DMA object are affected; for
  every other device acpi_dma_get_range() returns -ENODEV and nothing is
  touched. Firmware without _DMA, which is nearly all x86 firmware,
  sees no change.
- For affected devices without an IOMMU, dma-direct and swiotlb return
  translated addresses, and a physical address that no range covers
  fails to map (DMA_MAPPING_ERROR) instead of being passed through.
- For affected devices behind an IOMMU DMA domain, iommu-dma ignores the
  table but honours bus_dma_limit as an upper IOVA bound, as on arm64.
- The table is allocated per probe by acpi_dma_get_range() and freed by
  the driver core on unbind or probe failure (device_unbind_cleanup()).

SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-2.0-only
Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
