DRAFT -- notes for the submitter, not part of the commit message:

- Base: Ubuntu noble kernel tag Ubuntu-6.8.0-136.136 (9e2939f04a28),
  the guest kernel of the Caraxes VM. The three touched files are
  identical to upstream v6.8 there, so the patch also applies to v6.8.
- Compile-tested only: drivers/acpi/x86/dma.o and drivers/acpi/scan.o
  build without warnings (W=1, GCC 16.2.1) with x86_64_defconfig and
  with Ubuntu's generic amd64 config exported from
  debian.master/config/annotations; vmlinux links with x86_64_defconfig
  (CONFIG_WERROR off: GCC 16 trips unrelated -Werror checks in v6.8).
  Not boot-tested; nothing here has run.
- checkpatch: code clean; it asks for a Signed-off-by (to be added by
  the submitter), flags the Co-Authored-By spelling and the
  SPDX-License-Identifier trailer (both to be adjusted or dropped
  before posting), and asks whether MAINTAINERS needs updating
  (drivers/acpi/x86/ is covered by the ACPI entry).
- v6.12 and mainline (read at v7.3-rc4): same change, two differences.
  drivers/acpi/x86/ has its own Makefile there, so the object is added
  as "acpi-x86-y += dma.o" in drivers/acpi/x86/Makefile instead of
  drivers/acpi/Makefile; and the loop computing the highest bus address
  becomes dma_range_map_max(map) (include/linux/dma-direct.h, since
  v6.10). The include/linux/acpi.h hunk is unchanged. That variant was
  compile-tested on v6.12 (defconfig, W=1); it was not built on
  mainline.
- Review questions to expect upstream:
  1. x86 firmware that ships a _DMA object today, so far ignored, would
     start to be honoured. If that turns out to exist and be wrong,
     gate the x86 path (for example on the PCI host bridge only, or
     behind a Kconfig/cmdline switch).
  2. arm64, LoongArch and now x86 carry nearly the same body; a generic
     helper in drivers/acpi/scan.c with arm64 adding its IORT fallback
     may be preferred over a third copy.
  3. dma-direct's zone heuristic (dma_direct_optimal_gfp_mask) uses
     dma_to_phys(bus_dma_limit); with a table whose highest bus range
     maps to low guest memory, coherent allocations are steered to
     ZONE_DMA32. Correct, but conservative; same on arm64 today.

